    <div class="narrative prose">
      <SectionHeading id="choose">Choose your Buenos Aires</SectionHeading>
      <p>
        With one day, stay central: Plaza de Mayo, the Obelisk, Teatro Colón and El Ateneo
        make a coherent introduction, with Recoleta added only if time remains. With three
        days, give one day to the civic and cultural centre, one to Recoleta and Palermo,
        and one to San Telmo, Puerto Madero and La Boca. With five nights, slow the museum
        choices down and leave room for a separate Colonia del Sacramento day trip.
      </p>
      <p>
        Buenos Aires is large enough that the order matters. A day built around nearby
        institutions feels different from one built around neighbourhood movement. The
        <a href="/5-days-in-buenos-aires-itinerary/">five-day Buenos Aires itinerary</a>
        supplies the full chronology; the <a href="/buenos-aires-neighbourhoods/">Buenos Aires neighbourhoods guide</a>
        is the better companion when the decision is where to spend a slower day.
      </p>
    </div>

    <Chapter id="historic-centre" number="I." name="Start with Plaza de Mayo and the historic centre" epithet="Where political Buenos Aires is easiest to read" />
    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        Plaza de Mayo is the right first stop because it is not simply a square surrounded
        by attractive buildings. It is the oldest public square in Buenos Aires and the
        place where the city’s political life has repeatedly made itself visible. Its name
        recalls the May Revolution of 1810, the beginning of the independence process, while
        the buildings around it hold several earlier layers of the city.
      </p>
      <p>
        The Casa Rosada occupies the site of the old Buenos Aires fort and remains the seat
        of the national government. Across the square, the Metropolitan Cathedral carries
        the religious history of the centre, and the Cabildo keeps the colonial civic world
        within the same view. The Pirámide de Mayo, raised in 1811 and later moved to its
        present position, gives the open space a physical memory of the revolution rather
        than leaving that history inside a museum.
      </p>
      <p>
        From here, Avenida de Mayo leads towards the Plaza del Congreso and the more
        monumental civic architecture of the capital. The Manzana de las Luces adds a
        different register: institutions of learning and religion gathered within the old
        centre’s dense blocks. The <a href="/plaza-de-mayo-buenos-aires/">focused Plaza de Mayo guide</a>
        is worth reading alongside this wider list because the square rewards attention to
        its political continuities, not just a photograph of the pink palace.
      </p>
    </div>

    <Figure
      src={`${IMG}/buenos-aires-civic-architecture.jpg`}
      srcset={`${IMG}/buenos-aires-civic-architecture-480w.jpg 480w, ${IMG}/buenos-aires-civic-architecture-768w.jpg 768w, ${IMG}/buenos-aires-civic-architecture-1200w.jpg 1200w, ${IMG}/buenos-aires-civic-architecture-1600w.jpg 1600w, ${IMG}/buenos-aires-civic-architecture.jpg 2048w`}
      sizes={SIZES_MODERN_WIDE}
      alt="The National Congress building in Buenos Aires viewed from its surrounding park."
      width={2048}
      height={1365}
      caption="The central route broadens from Plaza de Mayo towards the Congress and the monumental city."
    />

    <Chapter id="obelisk" number="II." name="See the Obelisk and Avenida 9 de Julio" epithet="A modern city marker rather than a stop that needs a whole afternoon" />
    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        The Obelisk is not a destination so much as a punctuation mark in the middle of
        Buenos Aires. It stands where Avenida Corrientes meets Avenida 9 de Julio, the
        broad avenue named for the date of Argentina’s declaration of independence. The
        monument was designed by Alberto Prebisch and inaugurated in 1936 to mark the
        fourth centenary of the city’s first foundation.
      </p>
      <p>
        Its importance is urban. The Obelisk makes the scale of the modern capital visible,
        while the theatre, towers, traffic and side streets around it keep the monument
        from becoming an isolated object. The original controversy around its modern form
        has long given way to recognition: this is now one of the city’s defining markers.
        Stop for the view, then keep moving towards Teatro Colón, Palacio Barolo, Galerías
        Pacífico or the cultural institutions around Avenida de Mayo.
      </p>
    </div>

    <Chapter id="teatro-colon" number="III." name="Make time for Teatro Colón" epithet="The city’s grandest cultural institution in the central route" />
    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        Teatro Colón gives the central day an interior scale. The current building opened
        in 1908, and its history belongs to the period when Buenos Aires was building the
        cultural institutions of a capital that wanted to be taken seriously on an
        international stage. Its architecture draws the visitor inward through the foyer,
        staircases, sculpture and decorated rooms before the auditorium makes the full
        argument for grandeur.
      </p>
      <p>
        The theatre’s importance is not only architectural. It remains a working opera,
        ballet and concert institution, with an artistic training programme and an active
        relationship to Argentine cultural life. A guided visit is the practical way to
        understand the building when there is no performance that fits the stay; a ticket
        for the programme makes it something more than a beautiful shell. Check the
        theatre’s official visitor arrangements before going, since tours and performances
        follow their own schedules.
      </p>
    </div>

    <Chapter id="el-ateneo" number="IV." name="Step inside El Ateneo Grand Splendid" epithet="A bookstore that still behaves architecturally like a theatre" />
    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        El Ateneo Grand Splendid changes the central route without abandoning its cultural
        theme. The building began as the Teatro Grand Splendid, and its theatre shell still
        governs the experience: balconies frame the room, the former stage becomes a place
        to sit with a book, and the decorative details survive beneath the language of a
        modern bookstore.
      </p>
      <p>
        That is why it deserves more than a quick photograph. Buenos Aires has a serious
        relationship with books, newspapers, cafés and public argument, and El Ateneo gives
        that literary culture an unusually legible building. It works naturally after
        Teatro Colón or Palacio Barolo, when the day has already moved from government to
        performance and can now turn towards reading.
      </p>
    </div>

    <Chapter id="recoleta" number="V." name="Give Recoleta a cultural day" epithet="Cemetery, museums and the grander northern city" />
    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        Recoleta is strongest when treated as a district rather than a cemetery with a few
        extras attached. Recoleta Cemetery is the famous centrepiece, but the surrounding
        cultural institutions explain why the area became one of Buenos Aires’ most
        concentrated addresses for art, memory and public life.
      </p>
      <p>
        The Museo Nacional de Bellas Artes provides the broadest introduction to European
        and Argentine art. The Museo Nacional de Arte Decorativo narrows the focus to
        interiors, objects and the social world that produced them, while MALBA takes the
        route towards modern and contemporary Latin American art. Floralis Genérica gives
        the district a large piece of public sculpture and a more open park setting between
        the museum visits.
      </p>
      <p>
        The cemetery belongs in the same cultural reading. Its mausoleums turn the history
        of Argentine public life into an urban landscape, but it does not need to be rushed
        through as a checklist of famous names. Choose the museums that genuinely interest
        you, then leave enough time for the district’s avenues and open spaces to make sense
        of the institutions.
      </p>
    </div>

    <Figure
      src={`${IMG}/buenos-aires-floralis-generica.jpg`}
      srcset={`${IMG}/buenos-aires-floralis-generica-480w.jpg 480w, ${IMG}/buenos-aires-floralis-generica-768w.jpg 768w, ${IMG}/buenos-aires-floralis-generica-1200w.jpg 1200w, ${IMG}/buenos-aires-floralis-generica-1600w.jpg 1600w, ${IMG}/buenos-aires-floralis-generica.jpg 2048w`}
      sizes={SIZES_MODERN_WIDE}
      alt="Floralis Genérica in the Recoleta area of Buenos Aires."
      width={2048}
      height={1365}
      caption="Floralis Genérica gives Recoleta’s museum route a wide, open punctuation."
    />

    <Chapter id="san-telmo" number="VI." name="Walk San Telmo’s market streets" epithet="Older urban fabric, market culture and the city’s tango inheritance" />
    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        San Telmo works through street life and older urban fabric. It is one of Buenos
        Aires’ oldest neighbourhoods, and its low houses, cobbled streets, courtyards and
        antiques shops carry the history of the southern edge of the centre. The district
        changed sharply after the 1871 yellow-fever epidemic pushed many wealthy families
        north, leaving a different social and architectural life to develop among the old
        buildings.
      </p>
      <p>
        Mercado de San Telmo is the practical starting point. Its iron structure, food
        stalls, cafés, antiques and everyday shopping make it more useful than a market
        treated only as scenery. From there, Defensa leads towards Plaza Dorrego, the
        neighbourhood’s central square and the setting for the Sunday antiques fair, while
        Museo Moderno gives the day an art institution rather than another street view.
      </p>
      <p>
        San Telmo also belongs to the history of tango, even when the visit does not include
        a performance. The district’s bars, dance halls and older streets form part of the
        urban culture from which tango became one of Buenos Aires’ defining exports. A
        visitor can choose a formal show, look for a milonga or simply understand the
        neighbourhood as one of the places where that tradition remains attached to the
        city rather than presented as a separate theatre product.
      </p>
    </div>

    <Chapter id="puerto-madero" number="VII." name="See Puerto Madero rewrite the waterfront" epithet="Converted docks, contemporary towers and art beside the river" />
    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        Puerto Madero is useful because it shows Buenos Aires changing its waterfront
        rather than preserving it unchanged. The former docklands have become a district of
        converted warehouses, footbridges, towers and open water, with the Puente de la
        Mujer providing its most recognisable piece of contemporary infrastructure.
      </p>
      <p>
        The change is clearest after San Telmo. A short distance separates the market’s
        older fabric from a neighbourhood built around redevelopment, office buildings and
        the modern river edge. The Amalia Lacroze de Fortabat Art Collection adds a private
        art institution to the walk; the silos and waterfront buildings make the architecture
        itself part of the visit. Puerto Madero does not need to become a dining or nightlife
        guide. Its value is the contrast between inherited city and constructed city.
      </p>
    </div>

    <Chapter id="la-boca" number="VIII." name="Follow La Boca from port to football" epithet="Immigrant history, Caminito, art and the home of Boca Juniors" />
    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        La Boca carries Buenos Aires back towards the port. Its history is tied to immigrant
        workers, especially the Genoese communities that shaped the neighbourhood’s language,
        labour and built form. The painted corrugated façades around Caminito are the most
        recognisable expression of that history, but the district is larger than the
        postcard street.
      </p>
      <p>
        The route through Caminito, Pasaje Garibaldi, the Puente Transbordador and Fundación
        Proa moves between popular art, industrial memory and the contemporary art world.
        La Bombonera adds the city’s football culture to the same geography. Boca Juniors is
        not merely a team with a stadium in the neighbourhood: the club’s identity is bound
        to La Boca, its colours and its working port history. The stadium stop belongs in a
        first visit even without a match. Match attendance should not be assumed, but the
        place of football in the neighbourhood cannot be separated from the visit.
      </p>
    </div>

    <Chapter id="palermo" number="IX." name="Let Buenos Aires open out in Palermo" epithet="Parks, museums and the green exhale of the city" />
    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        Palermo changes the city’s pace without leaving the city behind. The district has
        room for parks, gardens, wide avenues and the long walks that the denser centre does
        not offer. El Rosedal gives the green route its formal heart, while the Planetario
        Galileo Galilei supplies an architectural marker that feels entirely different from
        the civic monuments south of the centre.
      </p>
      <p>
        Plaza Serrano and Palermo Soho bring a more commercial, contemporary side of the
        district, while Paseo de la Infanta runs through the park edge with a different
        relationship to the city. Palermo is not simply a place to add after the serious
        attractions are finished. It is where the guide’s argument about urban scale becomes
        visible: Buenos Aires can move from government, markets and football into gardens
        without becoming a different city.
      </p>
    </div>

    <Chapter id="museums" number="X." name="Choose the museums that fit the day" epithet="A cultural network rather than a checklist" />
    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        Buenos Aires has too many worthwhile museums for a useful guide to pretend that all
        of them belong on one list. Choose by interest and geography instead. Museo Nacional
        de Bellas Artes is the broadest choice for European and Argentine art, and fits
        naturally with Recoleta. MALBA is the stronger choice for Latin American modern and
        contemporary art. Museo Nacional de Arte Decorativo suits visitors more interested
        in interiors, objects and social history.
      </p>
      <p>
        Museo Moderno belongs with San Telmo and the southern centre. The Fortabat collection
        works with Puerto Madero, while Palacio Libertad and Galerías Pacífico can be folded
        into a central architectural day when their cultural programmes or interiors appeal.
        The point is not to rank collections from a distance. It is to choose one or two
        museums that deepen the neighbourhood already underfoot.
      </p>
    </div>

    <SectionHeading id="tango">Tango belongs in the Buenos Aires conversation</SectionHeading>
    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        Tango should not disappear from a first-visit guide simply because the traveller did
        not attend a performance on this stay. It grew from the working-class districts and
        port culture of Buenos Aires and Montevideo, then became one of the city’s defining
        cultural forms. San Telmo and La Boca provide the historical geography; milongas and
        theatre shows provide two different ways to encounter it now.
      </p>
      <p>
        A formal dinner-and-show suits a visitor who wants an arranged evening. A milonga is
        a better fit for someone interested in the social life of the dance. Neither should
        be described as a first-hand experience here. The useful advice is to choose the
        format deliberately rather than treating every tango venue as interchangeable.
      </p>
    </div>

    <SectionHeading id="food">Eat through the city’s Italian and Argentine inheritance</SectionHeading>
    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        Food is another way Buenos Aires changes register. Parrillas connect the city to
        Argentine beef and the ritual of the shared grill. Empanadas belong to a wider
        national tradition, while pizza, fugazzeta, cafés, medialunas and helado show the
        depth of Italian influence in the capital’s everyday eating.
      </p>
      <p>
        The best use of this guide is not to turn those categories into a restaurant ranking.
        Put a parrilla or pizzeria into a neighbourhood day, leave room for a café, and let
        the food follow the city rather than interrupting it. Buenos Aires is particularly
        good at making an ordinary meal feel like part of the urban argument: old port,
        immigrant kitchen, formal café, modern district.
      </p>
    </div>

    <Practical place="Buenos Aires — Things to Do" detail="Ten experiences · five nights" detailIcon="🧭">
      <PracticalSection label="Shortest route to a great first visit" level={3}>
        <div class="prac-item"><strong>One day</strong> — Plaza de Mayo, Obelisk, Teatro Colón and El Ateneo. Add Recoleta only if the day still has room.</div>
        <div class="prac-item"><strong>Three days</strong> — One day for the civic and cultural centre, one for Recoleta and Palermo, one for San Telmo, Puerto Madero and La Boca.</div>
        <div class="prac-item"><strong>Five nights</strong> — Slow the museum choices down and leave room for tango, longer neighbourhood walks and a separate Colonia day trip.</div>
        <div class="prac-item"><strong>If you only choose three things</strong> — Plaza de Mayo, one major cultural institution and one neighbourhood outside the centre.</div>
      </PracticalSection>
      <PracticalSection label="What each part of the city does best" level={3}>
        <div class="prac-item"><strong>Politics and history:</strong> Plaza de Mayo and the historic centre.</div>
        <div class="prac-item"><strong>Theatre and books:</strong> Teatro Colón and El Ateneo.</div>
        <div class="prac-item"><strong>Museums and monuments:</strong> Recoleta.</div>
        <div class="prac-item"><strong>Markets, port and football:</strong> San Telmo, Puerto Madero and La Boca.</div>
        <div class="prac-item"><strong>Parks and slower city time:</strong> Palermo.</div>
      </PracticalSection>
      <PracticalSection label="Watch-outs" level={3}>
        <div class="tip-row"><strong>Do not compress the whole city into one day.</strong> Buenos Aires is large enough that bad routing can turn the visit into transfers.</div>
        <div class="tip-row"><strong>Choose museums by district.</strong> One or two strong institutions are better than trying to collect them all.</div>
        <div class="tip-row"><strong>Treat Colonia as a separate day-trip decision.</strong> It is not another Buenos Aires neighbourhood.</div>
        <div class="tip-row"><strong>Do not treat tango as one product.</strong> A formal show and a milonga are different experiences.</div>
      </PracticalSection>
    </Practical>

    <SectionHeading id="priorities">What to prioritise</SectionHeading>
    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        <strong>With one day:</strong> begin at Plaza de Mayo and walk towards the Obelisk,
        Teatro Colón and El Ateneo. If the museums matter more than the central interiors,
        replace one stop with Recoleta. Do not try to add La Boca or Palermo unless the day
        is unusually long; the city’s scale is part of what makes the choice meaningful.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>With three days:</strong> give the first day to Plaza de Mayo, Avenida 9 de
        Julio, Teatro Colón and El Ateneo. Use the second for Recoleta and Palermo, choosing
        the museum that best fits the interest of the day. Use the third for San Telmo,
        Puerto Madero and La Boca, allowing the older market streets to lead into the
        converted docks and then the football-and-port identity of the south.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>With five days:</strong> follow the <a href="/5-days-in-buenos-aires-itinerary/">complete five-day Buenos Aires itinerary</a>
        for the fuller chronology, but keep the hub’s freedom to slow down. The extra time
        is best spent on a second museum, a tango evening, a longer Palermo walk or a
        separate day trip to Colonia del Sacramento rather than on adding more landmarks
        to a single day.
      </p>
    </div>

    <SectionHeading id="colonia">With an extra day: Colonia del Sacramento</SectionHeading>
    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        Colonia del Sacramento in Uruguay is one of the classic extensions from Buenos
        Aires. It is not another Buenos Aires neighbourhood and should remain a separate
        day-trip decision. Keep the old quarter, waterfront and practical crossing questions
        separate from the Buenos Aires visit, and check current ferry details before travel.
      </p>
    </div>

    <SectionHeading id="closing">Buenos Aires in changing registers</SectionHeading>
    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        Buenos Aires does not settle into one image. Plaza de Mayo gives way to a theatre,
        the theatre to a bookstore, Recoleta to museums and gardens, museums to San Telmo’s
        market streets, markets to converted docks, docks to football streets, and finally
        the city loosens into Palermo’s parks. Its pleasure lies not in finding the one
        monument that explains it, but in noticing how often it changes character without
        ceasing to be Buenos Aires.
      </p>
      <p>
        For the longer stay, continue to the <a href="/5-days-in-buenos-aires-itinerary/">five-day Buenos Aires itinerary</a>.
        For a slower comparison of the districts, use the <a href="/buenos-aires-neighbourhoods/">Buenos Aires neighbourhoods guide</a>.
      </p>
      <p>But the road is ever onward.</p>
    </div>

